# nr-exthost-pdf-document corrective integration

## Scope and ordering

Integrate the corrective mixdog-pdf-codec sources and tests together with mixdog-pdf parser.rs, stream.rs,
codec_glue.rs, page.rs, and the updated/new tests. Do not copy unrelated stale worktree files.
The syntax and codec API changes must land before downstream font/text consumers compile.
The existing document.rs remains the document/page-tree implementation; no production Node fallback is added.

## Exact dependency edge (lead-owned)

In native/core/crates/mixdog-pdf/Cargo.toml, [dependencies], add:
    mixdog-js = { workspace = true }

No external dependency is introduced. mixdog-js is foundational and has no PDF dependency (checked in both
this worktree and the integration tree). Use its existing number_to_string/string_to_number implementations.
Keep the existing brotli, mixdog-pdf-codec, tracing, flate2, mixdog-testkit, and serde_json edges.
Regenerate Cargo.lock offline as needed without --locked; do not replace its package/dependency blocks with
this older worktree's lockfile. No Cargo or Rust build/test command was run by the implementing agent.

## API consumers

- Codec ascii85::decode, asciihex::decode, runlength::decode, and lzw::decode now return Result<Vec<u8>, CodecError>.
  Flate/predictor/decode_chain retain Result but can return CodecError::ResourceLimit { limit }.
  ResourceLimit is fatal and must never be converted to an empty stream or a successful prefix.
- security::SecurityError adds ResourceLimit { limit }; EncryptDict's stm_f/str_f/eff become
  Option<CryptFilterName>, preserving Name versus truthy non-Name. From<String>/From<&str> support literal callers.
  Existing vector callers have been updated. The transform exposes narrow selector/prepared-CF methods for
  the structure adapter's lazy resolution. No office-crypto PDF security consumer exists in this worktree or
  the inspected integration tree; future consumers must propagate this Result contract.
- Decryptor::decrypt_string(..., xref: Option<&Xref>) -> Result<Vec<u8>>.
  Decryptor::validate_stream(..., embedded_file: bool, xref: Option<&Xref>) -> Result<()> has a default no-op.
  Decryptor::uses_embedded_filter() -> bool defaults false; version 4/5 adapters override it.
  Decryptor::decrypt_stream gains embedded_file: bool. The parser passes context only while selecting a cipher;
  streams store no Xref reference. Each object owns one constructor cache; no mutex is held during indirect CF fetch.
  The in-tree Xor test implementation is updated. No other implementations were found.
- Page.rotate and Viewport.rotation are Obj, preserving numeric strings/arrays as returned by pdf.js.
  Page.viewport(scale) still returns Viewport. Viewport::new takes Obj and returns Result<Viewport>, rejecting
  invalid explicit rotations. The in-tree structure twin compares type as well as value. No other callers found.
  This patch does not add the broader PageViewport cloning/conversion/dontFlip API.

## Evidence and required lead verification

Original Node v22.20.0 / pdf.js 6.4.299 recorded 33 small PDFs in tests/document_followup.json:
14 Rotate/coercion cases and 19 encryption cases (unused malformed CF, non-dict selected CF, lazy string errors,
invalid selectors, EFF, explicit Crypt catch behavior). The recorder and original source/input SHA256 values
are stored in the fixture. The recorder rebuilds correct xrefs, rather than relying on recovery after mutation.
Its encrypted seed comes from the existing pdf-codec corpus and is embedded as complete tiny PDF hex inputs.
The encrypted text check validates decoded content presence, not the future text-layout extraction engine.
Additional tiny Rust regressions cover exact-boundary output, per-stage limits before predictor shrinking,
raw/decrypted streams, and parser default-versus-explicit Crypt fatal/nonfatal handling.

After integration and corpus placement, the lead should run targeted tests for mixdog-pdf-codec and mixdog-pdf
(including vectors, syntax, document_followup, structure_twin and codec_twin), then the appropriate lint check.
No budgets were lowered: existing object/depth/byte defaults are unchanged. Bounded decoders observe at most
one sentinel byte beyond the byte limit (or their existing small decoding block) and report typed overflow.

## Remaining product stages

fonts/mod.rs, text/mod.rs, fontfile/mod.rs, and inspect.rs are still placeholders owned by future tasks.
This is document/geometry/decoded-content support, not a complete PDF text/font engine or a service registration.
Do not remove the existing product path until those engine stages and the service wiring have passed their twins.


## Constructor-cache corrective review (pdf.js 6.4.299)

Also integrate the small xref.rs::cipher_for change with stream.rs's default
Decryptor::for_object(num, generation) hook. CodecDecryptor creates one transform
per object fetch/parse. The stream retains that object's transform; fetching an
object again starts a new cache, even for the same object ID.

security.rs caches CipherKind (constructor/key material), never an RC4/AES instance.
Its shared Arc cache survives temporary prepared-CF transform clones. Both string
and stream selection consult this cache before invalid-selector validation. A Name
uses its name as the key; any non-Name selector uses __default__. Thus a string
using /StrF /__default__ can prime a subsequent truthy non-Name StmF in the same
object. Every decrypt_string and decrypt_stream instantiates fresh cipher state.
The earlier adapter-wide stream HashMap is removed; there is only one constructor
cache implementation, owned by CipherTransform.

The adapter preserves whether /CF was absent versus explicit null. At lazy use,
missing gives "Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'get')", null gives
"Cannot read properties of null (reading 'get')", and /CF false gives
"this.cf.get is not a function". Its no-password optional AuthEvent access retains
"this.cf?.get is not a function" for a non-dictionary CF.

Nine new returning Node references cover the __default__ collision, isolation
between objects, consecutive AES strings and stream, missing/null/false CF,
unused false CF, and invalid EFF on ordinary versus EmbeddedFile streams.
The consecutive AES test also inflates each decrypted string and compares it to
the decoded stream, detecting accidental state sharing across these consumers.

Exact original source evidence: pdf.worker.mjs:63812-63843 CipherTransform,
#getCipher's Name-or-__default__ key and getOrInsertComputed before resolveCipher;
createStream/decryptString each use `new Cipher()`. Factory resolver at
64123-64153 validates Name only on a cache miss, then calls this.cf.get.
The original source SHA256 is pinned in document_followup.json. The Node oracle
was rerun; Rust compilation/tests remain lead-owned and were not run here.
